12.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: C

The circumflex accent (ˆ) has a range of historically attested orthographic roles in French, all of which can be documented from the 16th century onward. These include:

- marking the deletion of a following /s/ (or other sibilant) that had existed in both speech and writing (H);
- signalling the contraction of a former diphthong or hiatus sequence into a single vowel (G, I);
- distinguishing homophones that would otherwise be spelled identically (B);
- indicating vowel-quality contrasts (closed vs. open) that arose after those contractions (A);
- marking vowel length, at least in the prescriptive orthographies of the 17th–19th centuries (D);
- serving an etymological or visual-prestige function by evoking a Latin or Greek pedigree even when the phonological justification was weak (F).

Option C, however, does not correspond to any documented motivation. While many words containing ô do descend from Latin forms whose stressed vowel was /o/, the accent itself was never systematically applied on that etymological basis; its presence is invariably explained by one of the other functions listed above. Consequently C is the function that has never been attested.

(The remaining choice, E, is therefore ruled out.)

